course: books
lesson: 38
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IV, part 2: Mr. Badger (bad news of Toad, a winter promise, and two lost hedgehogs)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Full and warm by the fire, the friends chat, and Badger asks how old Toad is getting on. «From bad to worse,» says the Rat. Toad has smashed his seventh motor-car; his coach-house is piled to the roof with bits of cars; he has been in hospital three times and paid huge fines. Sooner or later he will be killed or ruined.
explain: The Rat asks: shouldn't his friends do something? Badger says he can't do anything now — it's winter, and in winter no animal is expected to do anything brave or busy. But when the year turns and the nights get shorter, the three of them will «take Toad seriously in hand» and make him a sensible Toad. Halfway through his speech, he notices the Rat is asleep.
explain: The Mole feels lively underground — he is a digger, and Badger's house suits him; the Rat, used to a breezy river bedroom, feels sleepy and shut in. Badger shows them to a room half full of apples, potatoes, nuts and honey, with two little white beds smelling of lavender.
explain: Next morning they come down very late and find two young hedgehogs eating porridge. They got lost in the snow on the way to school and knocked at Badger's back door. And where is Badger? «Busy» in his study, they say — which everyone knows means asleep in his arm-chair with a red handkerchief over his face.
fact: After supper each animal «felt that his skin was now as tight as was decently safe» — a funny way of saying they were stuffed full.
fact: Badger asks «How's old Toad going on?» The Rat: «Oh, from bad to worse … Another smash-up only last week, and a bad one.»
fact: The trouble: Toad «will insist on driving himself, and he's hopelessly incapable» (incapable = not able to do it). He thinks he is «a heaven-born driver» whom nobody can teach. If he'd only pay «a decent, steady, well-trained animal» to drive for him, «he'd get on all right».
fact: Badger asks «Smashes, or machines?» The Rat: «it's the same thing—with Toad. This is the seventh.» Toad's coach-house is «piled up to the roof—with fragments of motor-cars, none of them bigger than your hat!» The Mole adds: «He's been in hospital three times», and the fines are «simply awful».
fact: The Rat: «Toad's rich, we all know; but he's not a millionaire … Killed or ruined—it's got to be one of the two things, sooner or later.»
fact: Badger: «of course you know I can't do anything now?» — by «animal-etiquette» (the animals' rules of good behaviour), nobody does anything «strenuous, or heroic» in the winter «off-season»; all are sleepy, some actually asleep.
fact: The plan: when «the year has really turned», «we'll take Toad seriously in hand. We'll stand no nonsense whatever … We'll make him be a sensible Toad.» Then: «We'll—you're asleep, Rat!» «Not me!» The Mole laughs that the Rat has been asleep «two or three times since supper».
fact: The Mole is wide awake because he is «an underground animal by birth and breeding»; the Rat, whose bedroom windows open «on a breezy river», finds the air «still and oppressive» (oppressive = heavy and stuffy).
fact: The bedroom is «half bedchamber and half loft», half full of Badger's winter stores: apples, turnips, potatoes, nuts and jars of honey. The beds are clean and smell «beautifully of lavender». Rat and Mole were out of their clothes and into bed in about thirty seconds.
fact: Two young hedgehogs eat «oatmeal porridge out of wooden bowls», stand up and duck their heads «respectfully» when Rat and Mole come in.
fact: The elder hedgehog: «Me and little Billy here, we was trying to find our way to school—mother would have us go, was the weather ever so». In Standard English: «Billy and I were trying to find our way to school — Mother insisted, whatever the weather.» «We was» is his dialect, not a mistake in the book.
fact: «Billy he got frightened and took and cried, being young and faint-hearted» (faint-hearted = easily scared). He adds: «Mr. Badger he's a kind-hearted gentleman, as everyone knows». They «made so bold as to knock» (dared to knock) at Badger's back door.
fact: The Rat tells him «You needn't ‘sir’ me quite so much». In the old days poor children were taught to call richer people «sir»; today we'd say everyone deserves the same respect.
fact: «The master's gone into his study … on no account was he to be disturbed.» Really, Badger is in an arm-chair «with his legs up on another and a red cotton handkerchief over his face», being «busy» in the usual winter way.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «skint» (UK) = with no money left; «rizz» = charm; «canny» (North East England) = nice, or quite; «cwtch» (Wales) = a cuddle, a cosy corner; «y'all» (southern US) = you all.
exercise: What does the Badger ask about, once supper is over? | How old Toad is going on | Whether the weasels chased them | What the river is like in winter | Who taught the Mole to row
exercise: «From bad to worse.» What does the Rat mean? | Toad is getting worse and worse | Toad has been ill in bed with a cold for a week | Toad has moved to a worse house | The weather is getting worse
exercise: Which smash-up is this, as the Rat counts? | The seventh | The third, after two very small ones | The very first one | The twentieth, at least
exercise: What is Toad's coach-house full of? | Bits of motor-cars, none bigger than your hat | Brand-new boats and a yellow caravan | Hay for the old grey horse | Nothing — it is empty and swept clean, because Toad sold everything
exercise: How many times has Toad been in hospital, says the Mole? | Three times | Once, for a whole year after the first car | Seven times, once for every car | Never, not even once
exercise: What is Toad's big mistake about driving, according to the Rat? | He thinks he's a heaven-born driver and won't be taught | He drives far too slowly and holds up all the carts and horses on the high road | He lets the old grey horse drive | He only drives at night
exercise: What would make Toad «get on all right», says the Rat? | Paying a steady, well-trained animal to drive | Buying a bigger, stronger and more expensive car | Driving only on Sundays | Going back to rowing
exercise: «Toad's rich … but he's not a millionaire.» Why does the Rat say this? | Fines and smashed cars could ruin him | Toad wants to become a millionaire by selling cars | Toad gave all his money to the Badger | Toad has just won a prize
exercise: What two ends does the Rat fear for Toad? | Killed or ruined | Prison or the hospital, for many years | Lost or forgotten | Married or famous
exercise: Why can't the Badger do anything about Toad right now? | It's winter, and nobody does anything strenuous in winter | He is too old and tired, and has given up on Toad | He doesn't like Toad at all and won't help | He is ill
exercise: «Animal-etiquette» says that in winter… | Nobody is expected to do anything heroic or busy | Everybody must visit their friends every single day | All animals must leave the wood | Nobody may sleep
exercise: When will the three of them take Toad in hand? | When the year has turned and the nights are shorter | Tomorrow morning, straight after breakfast | Next Christmas | When Toad asks them
exercise: «We'll make him be a sensible Toad. We'll—» What interrupts the Badger? | He sees the Rat is asleep | The front-door bell clangs | The fire goes out | The Mole laughs at him
exercise: How many times has the Rat been asleep since supper, says the Mole? | Two or three times | Not once, he's wide awake and talking | At least ten times over | Only now, for the first time
exercise: Why does the Mole feel so wide awake in Badger's house? | He is an underground animal, so it feels like home | He drank a lot of strong coffee at supper with the Badger | He is still frightened of the Wild Wood | He slept in the hollow tree
exercise: Why does the Rat feel sleepy there? | He is used to a breezy river bedroom | He walked further than the Mole today | He ate the most | He never sleeps in winter
exercise: What fills half of the bedroom? | Badger's winter stores: apples, turnips, potatoes, nuts, honey | Badger's books and maps of the Wild Wood and the river | Spare beds for lost animals | Firewood
exercise: What do the clean sheets smell of? | Lavender | Smoke from the kitchen fire, and onions | Fresh, cold snow | Old apples and dust
exercise: How quickly do Rat and Mole get into bed? | In about thirty seconds | After an hour of talking | After a bath | They sit up all night
exercise: Who is at the kitchen table when Rat and Mole come down late? | Two young hedgehogs eating porridge | The Otter, eating fried ham, and the Badger | The Badger, reading the newspaper | Nobody at all
exercise: What do the hedgehogs do when the two come in? | Drop their spoons, stand up and duck their heads | Hide under the table and squeak | Run home | Ask for more porridge
exercise: Where were the hedgehogs going when they got lost? | To school | To their granny's house on the far side of the Wild Wood | Home from a party | To see Toad's motor-car
exercise: Why did they go out in the snow at all? | Their mother would have them go, whatever the weather | They wanted to play in the snow all day long | Badger sent for them | They had run away from home
exercise: What did little Billy do when they got lost? | Got frightened and cried | Climbed a tall tree to look for the way out | Found the door himself | Fell asleep
exercise: Where is Badger «busy», says the hedgehog? | In his study | Out in the snow, clearing paths | Down in the cellar | Away at Toad Hall
exercise: What is the Badger really doing in his study? | Sleeping in his arm-chair with a red handkerchief over his face | Writing a long, angry letter to Toad about his motor-cars | Counting his money | Mending boots
test: Liam, a bit salty (cross): «Toad's only crashed once, it's no big deal.» Put it right. | It's his seventh smash, three hospital stays and awful fines | Right: he crashed the caravan once and has driven slowly and safely ever since | He's crashed twice, both times in the Wild Wood | He's never crashed — the Rat is lying
test: Sophie from Cardiff: «Badger's all cwtched up by the fire and says they'll sort Toad out tomorrow.» (cwtched up = snug) Put it right. | Not tomorrow — when winter is over and the nights get shorter | Right: they all set off for Toad Hall first thing in the morning, straight after breakfast | He says they'll never sort Toad out | He says he'll go alone, at once
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Badger tells the Rat and the Mole off for not helping Toad sooner.» | False: he just thinks hard and plans for the spring | True: he shouts at them for a long time and bangs the table with his paw | True, and he blames the Mole | False: he refuses to talk about Toad
test: «He's rich, but if he keeps paying fines he'll be skint.» (skint = with no money) Which earlier warning from the Rat says the same thing? | «Killed or ruined—it's got to be one of the two» | «It's never the wrong time to call on Toad, early or late, he's always the same» | «We don't go there very much» | «Look ahead, Rat!»
test: «Me and little Billy here, we was trying to find our way to school.» How would a school book say it? | Billy and I were trying to find our way to school | Me and Billy was trying to find school | Billy and me was trying for school | We was trying, me and Billy
test: Is the hedgehog's «we was» a mistake by the writer? | No — it's how the hedgehog speaks in his dialect | Yes, the writer forgot his grammar on this page | Yes, the printer made a mistake | No, «we was» is correct in a school book
test: «The master's gone into his study … on no account to be disturbed.» Why does everyone understand? | They know he's really having a winter snooze | They know he's writing an important book about the city | They know he's angry with them | They know he's gone out
test: Ava, lowkey impressed: «Badger's got rizz, even the hedgehogs know their way to his door.» (rizz = charm) What does the hedgehog say about Badger? | «Mr. Badger he's a kind-hearted gentleman, as everyone knows» | «Mr. Badger is very fierce and bites, so be careful when you knock on his door» | «Mr. Badger doesn't like children» | «We never heard of Mr. Badger»
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad do right after the motor-car smashed his caravan? | Sat in the road murmuring «Poop-poop!» in a happy dream | Shouted «You villains! You road-hogs!» at the driver and threw stones at the car | Helped Rat and Mole lift the cart | Ran home crying
test: Looking back: what was the last news of Toad at the end of chapter II? | He went up to Town and ordered a large, very expensive motor-car | He sold Toad Hall and bought a brand-new house-boat with all the money he got | He mended the yellow caravan | He went to live with the Badger
test: Looking back: before motor-cars, which crazes did Toad have, as the Rat told the Mole in chapter I? | Sailing, then punting, then house-boating | Fishing, then painting pictures, then playing music | Golf and tennis | Only rowing, for years
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Otter sum Toad up? | «Such a good fellow, too … But no stability—especially in a boat!» | «A clever animal, and very brave» | «Never trust him» | «He is a great sailor»
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what did the Rat do in winter at home? | He slept a great deal, and scribbled poetry | He went out hunting with the Otter every single night, whatever the weather | He rowed on the flooded river | He visited Toad every day
test: «The Mole … his heels higher than his head.» What does this picture show? | He is lying back, comfortable and happy, on the settle | He has fallen off the settle onto the floor and hurt his head | He is doing a handstand to show off | He is upside down in a hole
test: What would happen if Badger tried to save Toad in midwinter, against animal-etiquette? | Everyone is sleepy and snowed in, so it would be hard and odd | Toad would be grateful and give up motor-cars at once, for ever and ever | Nothing would change | The weasels would help
test: ★★★ «Smashes, or machines? … it's the same thing—with Toad.» Why are they the same for Toad? | Every car he buys, he smashes, so the counts match | He buys two new cars each time he smashes one of the old ones | He never buys cars, he borrows them | He smashes the same car seven times
test: ★★★ The Rat says the coach-house holds the «other six». The seventh smash was last week. How many cars has Toad had so far, if every car was smashed once? | 7 | 6 | 8 | 13
test: ★★★ «Then he's a millionaire,» says Theo, «because the Rat says he's rich.» What is wrong with Theo's reasoning? | The Rat says he's rich but «not a millionaire» — rich doesn't mean a millionaire | Nothing is wrong: rich people are always millionaires, everyone knows that | The Rat said Toad is poor | Toad gave his money away
exam: Ascertain the number of motor-car accidents attributed to Toad as at the time of the Rat's report. | Seven | Three, in respect of each of which he was admitted to hospital | Six in total | One only
exam: Identify the principal reason advanced by the Badger for the postponement of any intervention. | The winter season, in which no strenuous undertaking is expected of any animal | The absence of Toad from the district | His own indifference to Toad | The vehement opposition of the Rat to any interference whatsoever in the affairs of Toad
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hedgehogs' reason for their presence at the Badger's residence? | Having lost their way to school in the snow, they sought shelter | They had been formally invited to breakfast by the Badger on the preceding day | They were in the Badger's employ as cooks | They had been sent by the Otter
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Badger's occupation in his study that morning. | Repose in an arm-chair, with a handkerchief over his face | The composition of correspondence respecting Toad's conduct and affairs | Examination of his accounts | Absence from the house
